Short:        Skinned multiformat player and net radio
Author:       Darren Banfi (boingball)
Uploader:     boingball@gmail.com (Darren Banfi)
Type:         mus/play
Version:      2.0
Requires:     68030+; TCP/IP stack for radio; util/libs/AmiSSL-v5-OS3.lha for HTTPS
Architecture: m68k-amigaos >= 3.0.0


MintAMP - Mini Internet Amiga Media Player
==========================================

MintAMP is a lightweight multiformat audio player and internet radio client
for classic Amiga computers. It uses fixed-point decoders designed for m68k
systems and outputs directly through Paula using audio.device, so AHI and a
sound card are not required.

MintAMP 2.0 opens wearing a classic Winamp 2.x skin (.wsz), with a skinned
playlist, equaliser, artwork and settings windows, a spectrum analyser and
oscilloscope, and keyboard shortcuts you may already know. One menu tick
switches the running player to the classic MintAMP window and back again,
without stopping the music.

This archive contains two complete MintAMP drawers:

  MintAMP-v2.0-68030  For 68030 and 68040 systems
  MintAMP-v2.0-68060  Dedicated build for 68060 systems

Open or copy the drawer which matches the processor installed in your Amiga.
The 68060 edition contains dedicated 68060-safe and 68060-optimised decoder
paths. It is not intended for 68030 or 68040 processors.

What's New in 2.0
-----------------

  - Classic Winamp 2.x skin support, with the bundled mint-leaf skin
  - Skinned main, playlist, equaliser, artwork and settings windows
  - Live switch between the skin and the classic MintAMP window
  - Spectrum analyser and oscilloscope, synced to what you hear
  - MP3 equaliser, balance, shuffle/repeat and windowshade mode
  - MintAMP-Launch: one icon starting your preferred interface
  - Search and change radio stations while a stream is playing
  - Unplayable files now say why, in the status line and skin title
  - FLAC, Ogg, WMA, WAV and AAC files now play right to the end
  - WMA files with a video stream now play their audio
  - Faster 68060 MP3 and AAC decoding

See History at the end for the full list.

Which Program to Start
----------------------

Each drawer contains six programs:

  MintAMP-Launch        Start here. Opens the skin (or the classic
                        window) using the ReAction or GadTools edition
                        you have chosen in Interface preferences
  MintAMP-SR            Skin + classic window, ReAction utility windows
  MintAMP-SGT           Skin + classic window, GadTools utility windows
  MintAMP               Classic-only ReAction edition (no skin code)
  MintAMP-GT            Classic-only GadTools edition (no skin code)
  amiga_mp3dec.fastexp  Command-line edition

MintAMP-SR and MintAMP-SGT are the full 2.0 players. MintAMP and MintAMP-GT
are the smaller classic-only players, as in 1.4. Use a GadTools edition if
ReAction/ClassAct is not installed.

Skins
-----

Double-click MintAMP-Launch: the mint-leaf skin opens first. Right-click any
skin window (except the equaliser) for the MintAMP, Windows and Options
menus.

  - MintAMP > Classic interface switches to the classic MintAMP window.
    Untick Project > Classic interface there to return to the skin.
    Playback, the playlist and the radio browser carry on across the
    switch, and the choice is remembered for the next launch.
  - Windows > Load skin (S) loads any classic Winamp 2.x .wsz skin.
    Windows > Double size (D) doubles the skin.
  - Settings (T) opens a skinned settings window. Artwork (A) shows the
    album or station artwork in the skin's frame.
  - The minimise button iconifies all skin windows to a Workbench AppIcon;
    playback continues.

Main keys, as in Winamp:

  X play   C pause   V stop   Z previous   B next   O open file
  U open stream URL   R internet radio   I file info   E equaliser
  P playlist   J shuffle   L repeat   W windowshade
  Left/Right seek 5 s (local MP3)   Up/Down volume

The visualisation area cycles off, spectrum and oscilloscope when clicked.
Skins/ReadMe in each drawer describes every control and the limits of the
skin support.

Interface Preferences and ToolTypes
-----------------------------------

Use Interface preferences in any edition's menu to choose ReAction or
GadTools, Skin or Classic, and normal or double size for the next launch.
MintAMP-Launch --prefs opens the same choice from a Shell.

The MintAMP-Launch icon includes disabled examples of these ToolTypes:

  (GUI=REACTION)
  (MODE=CLASSIC)
  (SKIN=PROGDIR:Skins/MintAMP-mintleaf.wsz)
  (DOUBLESIZE=NO)

Remove the parentheses to force a setting. Command-line arguments override
ToolTypes, which override saved preferences, which override defaults. Leave
the examples disabled for your saved preferences to work.

Features
--------

  - Local MP3 playback using the Helix fixed-point decoder
  - Dedicated 68060 MP3 build with optimised synthesis, DCT, Huffman,
    mid/side stereo and planar sample conversion paths
  - Local and streamed AAC-LC ADTS/AAC+ playback
  - Local FLAC playback
  - Local Ogg Vorbis playback using the fixed-point Tremor decoder
  - Classic WMA playback (WMAv1/WMAv2 in ASF containers)
  - Uncompressed PCM WAV playback (8/16/24/32-bit, mono or stereo)
  - Amiga IFF-8SVX playback, including Fibonacci-delta compression
  - Bundled modular AAC, FLAC, Ogg, WMA, WAV and IFF decoders
  - Classic Winamp 2.x skins with playlist, equaliser, artwork and
    settings windows, visualisation and double size
  - HTTP and HTTPS internet radio: MP3, AAC/AAC+, Ogg Vorbis and lossless
    FLAC, with .pls/.m3u station links followed to the stream
  - Radio Browser station search and editable favourites, usable while a
    station is playing
  - Live ICY station, artist and title information
  - Station names and titles in any language shown in the Amiga's Latin-1
    fonts
  - Station artwork using JPEG, PNG, WebP, ICO and a subset of SVG
  - M3U and PLS playlists of files and radio stations
  - Iconify to a Workbench AppIcon while playback continues
  - ID3 tag display and track ratings
  - Configurable output rate, quality, channel mode and buffering
  - Reduced-rate and reduced-subband modes for slower systems
  - Direct Paula audio output; no AHI installation required

Requirements
------------

  - AmigaOS 3.0 or later
  - Motorola 68030 or better
  - Fast RAM is strongly recommended
  - A running TCP/IP stack providing bsdsocket.library for internet radio
  - AmiSSL v5 for HTTPS streams
  - ReAction/ClassAct v44 classes for MintAMP-SR and MintAMP

Local playback does not need a TCP/IP stack or AmiSSL.

Installation
------------

Extract the complete archive. Its root contains:

  MintAMP-v2.0-68030/
  MintAMP-v2.0-68060/
  MintAMP-v2.0-68030.info
  MintAMP-v2.0-68060.info

Choose the drawer matching your processor:

  68030 or 68040:
    Use MintAMP-v2.0-68030

  68060:
    Use MintAMP-v2.0-68060

You may leave both drawers together or copy only the required drawer to your
preferred location. Settings and favourites saved by MintAMP 1.x are kept.

Keep every file inside the selected drawer together. Its layout is:

  MintAMP-v2.0-68xxx/
    MintAMP-Launch
    MintAMP-SR
    MintAMP-SGT
    MintAMP
    MintAMP-GT
    amiga_mp3dec.fastexp
    (and their icons)
    ArtCache/
    Skins/
      MintAMP-mintleaf.wsz
      ReadMe
    decoders/
      aac.decoder
      flac.decoder
      ogg.decoder
      wma.decoder
      wav.decoder
      iff.decoder
    docs/

Start MintAMP-Launch from Workbench. For internet radio, start the TCP/IP
stack first. Install AmiSSL from util/libs/AmiSSL-v5-OS3.lha to play HTTPS
stations.

Command-Line Edition
--------------------

amiga_mp3dec.fastexp is the command-line edition. Open a Shell, change to the
selected MintAMP drawer and use --play followed by a local filename or direct
stream URL.

Examples:

  amiga_mp3dec.fastexp --play "Work:Music/example.mp3"
  amiga_mp3dec.fastexp --play "Work:Music/example.flac"
  amiga_mp3dec.fastexp --play "Work:Music/example.wma"
  amiga_mp3dec.fastexp --play "https://example.com/direct-stream"
  amiga_mp3dec.fastexp --play "http://example.com:8000/listen.pls?sid=1"

Useful options include:

  --rate 22050
  --mono
  --stereo
  --fast-lowrate
  --fast-mem
  --quality 0
  --subband-cap 16
  --buffer-seconds 4
  --volume 80

Run amiga_mp3dec.fastexp without arguments to display the complete option
list.

68060 Edition
-------------

The 68060 drawer is built for real 68060 hardware. It avoids instructions
which the 68060 must emulate in software and adds selected 68060-clean
assembly optimisations: polyphase synthesis and DCT, reduced-rate playback
paths, Huffman decoding, mid/side stereo and planar sample conversion, plus
hardware-only multiplies in the AAC, FLAC, Ogg and WMA decoders. It also
runs on FPU-less 68060 boards.

Do not use the 68030 decoder modules on a 68060: their full-result MULS.L
instructions are emulated and can be dramatically slower.

Internet Radio Notes
--------------------

MintAMP plays HTTP and HTTPS MP3, ADTS AAC/AAC+, Ogg Vorbis and FLAC
streams, and follows .pls/.m3u station links and redirects to the stream.
Press R in the skin (or Radio in the classic window) for the Internet Radio
browser. You can search, browse favourites and start another station while
one is playing; the status line shows Buffering, Streaming, Stopped or why
a stream failed.

Lossless FLAC stations play too, whether they send FLAC inside Ogg or plain
FLAC. Pick "FLAC" as the codec in the radio search to find them. A 16-bit
44.1 kHz FLAC station needs roughly 1 Mbit/s; a 68060 with a fast network
card is recommended.

A 128 kbit MP3 station is the safest choice for uninterrupted playback on
classic hardware. Streams at 192, 256 or 320 kbit may need a lower output
rate or may occasionally rebuffer.

HLS/M3U8 streams are not supported. Internet stations must provide a direct
MP3, ADTS AAC/AAC+, Ogg Vorbis or FLAC stream URL, or a .pls/.m3u playlist
naming one.

Playlists and Favourites
------------------------

The playlist (P in the skin, Playlist in the classic window) holds local
files and radio streams together and loads and saves .m3u, .m3u8 and .pls
files. From Favs and To Favs copy radio favourites to and from the list.

A playlist file can also be opened straight away: pick it in the file
requester, give it on the command line, or set its icon's Default Tool to
MintAMP-Launch. Started that way, it begins playing at once.

In the Internet Radio window, Favourites shows your saved stations. Add Fav
saves the selected or playing station; Rename, Remove and Move Up/Dn edit
the list. Up to 50 favourites are kept.

Format Notes
------------

AAC support is for ADTS files and direct ADTS/AAC+ streams. MP4/M4A files
are not supported.

FLAC decoding is considerably more demanding than MP3. Usable settings depend
on the processor, file complexity and selected output rate.

WMA support covers the classic WMAv1 and WMAv2 codecs in ASF containers,
including the audio of WMV/ASF files with video. WMA Pro, Lossless and Voice
are not supported; MintAMP says so when you try to play one.

Seeking works for local MP3 files. The equaliser applies to MP3 playback;
other formats bypass it.

Known Limitations
-----------------

  - Classic Winamp 2.x skins only: no modern WAL skins, Windows DLL
    plug-ins or Winamp visualisation plug-ins.
  - The classic-only MintAMP-GT window may not fit very small Workbench
    screens; the skinned editions and MintAMP do.
  - The GadTools radio browser needs a screen of at least 548x200.

Source Code
-----------

